Checked the source. **It's intended — and it's not a recent change at all.**

## The mechanism, from Codex's own code

`codex-rs/config/src/loader/mod.rs:1127`:

```rust
fn root_checkout_hooks_folder_for_dir(&self, dir: &AbsolutePathBuf) -> Option<AbsolutePathBuf> {
    let checkout_root = self.checkout_root.as_ref()?;
    let repo_root = self.repo_root.as_ref()?;
    // Regular checkouts resolve both paths to the same root; linked worktrees do not.
    if checkout_root == repo_root {
        return None;
    }
    let relative_dir = dir.as_path().strip_prefix(checkout_root.as_path()).ok()?;
    Some(repo_root.join(relative_dir).join(".codex"))
}
```

A dedicated function, with a comment naming linked worktrees explicitly. This is a deliberate `hooks_config_folder_override`, not an accident.

## It landed in May, with an explicit rationale

`934a40c7d9` — **openai/codex#21969, "Use root repo hooks in linked worktrees", 2026-05-13**, by Abhinav. That's the exact PR #2020 cited. From its body:

> Linked worktrees currently load their own project hook declarations, so the same repo can present different hook definitions depending on which checkout is active. [#21762] tried to share trust by giving matching worktree hooks a shared synthetic key, but review pointed out that divergent worktree hook definitions would then fight over one `trusted_hash`.
>
> Instead of introducing a second trust model, this makes linked worktrees use the root checkout as the single source of truth for project hook declarations.

So they considered the alternative, implemented it in #21762, and **rejected it on review**. This is load-bearing to Codex's hook trust model. It is not going to be casually reverted.

It's also confirmed as an ancestor of current HEAD, and nothing since May has touched that logic — the later `loader/mod.rs` commits are credential brokering, sandbox, managed config. So it's stable across 0.149.0 **and** 0.149.1, and has been for three months.

That PR body also confirms the mechanism your test documented, in Codex's own words: *"including linked-worktree layers with `.codex/` but no local `config.toml`"* — the worktree needs a `.codex/` to produce a project layer at all, and the override then redirects hook reads to the root checkout.

## What this changes

**On pinning:** an exact pin is over-constrained. The behavior is 3 months old, deliberate, and tied to a trust model they defended on review. A floor (`>=` the version that introduced it) is the honest constraint. 0.149.0 → 0.149.1 is a patch bump and almost certainly irrelevant here — nothing in the relevant path moved.

**On #2052's scope decision — this is the more important one.** Codex reads hooks from `repo_root/<relative>/.codex/hooks.json`, by design. So for Entire's hooks to fire in a linked worktree, they **must** be installed in the root checkout. There is no other location that works.

That means "diagnose, don't write to another checkout" isn't a conservative middle ground — it's a decision that **Entire doesn't support Codex in linked worktrees**, with the user hand-fixing every time. #2020 asked for install-to-authoritative-location precisely because that's the only thing Codex will read.

I'd put that back to Muskan directly: the refusal to write to the root checkout is principled, but Codex has already made the root checkout the single source of truth, so declining to write there means declining to work. Worth reconsidering now that the upstream intent is documented rather than inferred.

